// spec: a temporary array made by braces naming its type (`Three{1, 2, 3}` with `using Three = const int[3]`) where no reference to an array binds it: a `const int*` parameter takes its first element's address, the array living to the end of the full-expression ([conv.array], [class.temporary] 4), and an aggregate's reference member bound to one by braces keeps it as long as the aggregate ([class.temporary] 6). clang's and MSVC's output; g++ 13 refuses the pointer ("taking address of temporary array"), GCC's refusal of what [conv.array] materializes
// codegen made a temporary array only for a reference to an array (a function argument, a reference variable, a range-for's range) and refused one anywhere else until 2026-11-08 ("does not support a temporary array ... other than for a reference to an array"); its first version made one everywhere, and the aggregate's array was destroyed at the end of its statement, before the members were read. More in language/temporary_array_decays_to_a_pointer_argument and language/reference_member_bound_to_braces_makes_a_temporary
struct Noisy
{
  int v;
  implicit Noisy(int v) : v(v) { std::println("make ", v); }
  ~Noisy() { std::println("drop ", this.v); }
};
struct Holder { const Noisy (&r)[2]; };
int first(const int* p) { return p[0] * 10 + p[2]; }
using Three = const int[3];
using Two = const Noisy[2];
int main()
{
  std::println(first(Three{1, 2, 3}));
  {
    Holder h{Two{Noisy(3), Noisy(4)}};
    std::println("held ", h.r[0].v + h.r[1].v);
  }
  std::println("holder done");
  return 0;
}
